{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/150899-pookaknight-s-changeling-palooza","id":150899,"title":"PookaKnight's Changeling-palooza","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-27T17:14:09-07:00","id":150899,"title":"PookaKnight's Changeling-palooza","content":"We have (likely) just a few months until we find out if there will be a Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary edition or not. I can't even describe how excited I am to hear what news comes out of GenCon this year!<br /> <br /> To channel some of that excitement, I'm finally writing down some things I've been thinking about the game for years. If folks have fun with it, I'll keep going with other stuff that's in my head. My one request though, please don't complain that Dreaming isn't Lost or DA: Fae. There are plenty of other threads for that. This one is to celebrate Dreaming as Changeling: The Dream.<br /> <br /> First off, my take on Banality's rules: <hr />Changelings, their faerie souls composed of raw Dream stuff, are particularly susceptible to the ravages of Banality. After the Shattering, they enacted the Changeling Way Ritual to combine their souls with those of mortals as a buffer against the constant onslaught of mundanity. Unfortunately, their adopted humanity is not a perfect shield. Banality is insidious. Even the smallest lapse in vigilance is enough for it to erode the fantastic into dull mediocrity. The process of striping a changeling of his faerie heritage may be slow, but it is inevitable. No changeling has ever died of old age while still in her faerie mien.<br /> <br /> <b>Effects of Banality</b><br /> Banality is toxic to the Kithain. Once inside, it festers and spreads like cancer, slowly snuffing out the changeling\u2019s Glamour. As a changeling loses ever more contact with her faerie side, she has only her human life force to sustain her. The more Banality she accumulates, the more it physically takes its toll on her mortal seeming, ageing her prematurely. A permanently Undone changeling is often mistaken for a senile geriatric in the throws of dementia due to the vast swaths of memories she has lost.<br /> <br /> Banality clings the world and the people in it like a cold, wet blanket, chilling passions and snuffing out Glamour. To affect anything with her Glamour, a changeling must overcome this Banality. If a changeling attempts to cast a cantrip, enchant a mortal, call upon the wyrd, or take any other action that involves Glamour, the target\u2019s Banality always serves as the basis for the difficulty of the roll.<br /> <br /> Likewise, because changelings are acutely aware of the interplay between Banality and Glamour, a changeling may use her own accumulated Banality to negate Glamour around her. She may roll her own Banality to counter cantrips targeting her or call the Mists to her Banality so she may escape from chimerical reality temporarily.<br /> <br /> If a changeling\u2019s temporary Banality is higher than her permanent Glamour, the Mists may cloud her mind whether she wants them to or not. This tends to happen if a changeling goes for an extended period of time without contact with other changelings, chimera, or Glamour. Contact with anything of the Dreaming will usually clear the Mists once more.<br /> <br /> Fortunately, Banality acts as an insulator for itself. It is this that allows the human half of a changeling to be a shield for the faerie half. Consequently, the higher a changeling\u2019s permanent Banality the more severe actions she must take to open herself up to Banality\u2019s grasp. Of course, the apathy engendered by Banality also means that the more Banality she accumulates, the less likely she is to care.<br /> <br /> <b>The Death of Wonder</b><br /> When a changeling takes an action that invites Banality, consult the Acts of Banality chart. If the action is equal to or higher than the character\u2019s permanent Banality, the player must roll her character\u2019s permanent Glamour at a difficulty of 8 to counter the onslaught. If the roll fails, Banality manages to get an icy hook into the Kithain\u2019s heart in the way of a temporary Banality point. Once the character accumulates 10 temporary points, the chill of Banality extinguishes a little more of the changeling\u2019s vital spark. The temporary points are removed, and the character\u2019s permanent Banality increases by one.<br /> <br /> The Acts of Banality chart cannot anticipate every situation that may arise. If a character takes an action that seems as though it should invite Banality, the Storyteller should use her discretion to determine the action\u2019s permanent Banality rating. Likewise, if the Storyteller feels that a lower Act of Banality should be higher due to the nature of the circumstance, she may apply her own judgment.<br /> <br /> <b>Reawakening the Dream</b><br /> A changeling may stave off Banality temporarily through inspiring Glamour in others. When a changeling gains a temporary point of Glamour, she may immediately spend it to remove one temporary point of Banality. This may only be done at the time the temporary point of Glamour is gained.<br /> <br /> It is possible to thaw a changeling\u2019s soul after it becomes partially frozen by Banality, but not without great difficulty and risk. The changeling may embark on a quest to recover an item or being vital to the Dreaming, inspire a mortal to such heights he becomes an inspiration to other mortals, or find a way for an individual consumed by Banality to touch the Dreaming once more. The player should discuss the nature of the quest with the Storyteller. Whatever they agree on, the quest should somehow weaken Banality\u2019s grip on the world and give Glamour a little more room to flourish. Once the Storyteller gives her approval, the changeling must swear an oath to the Dreaming to undertake the quest. If the quest is successful, the player may spend 10 experience points to remove one permanent Banality point. If the quest ends in failure, the character automatically gains one permanent Banality point.<br /> <br /> <b>Acts of Banality</b><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"width: 500px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Permanent Banality</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Act</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Undone: The changeling is consumed by Banality and unable to access her faerie mien. The Mists cloud her mind and block all memory of the Dreaming.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Using Banality to negate cantrips or escape chimerical reality. Wielding cold iron.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Killing a mortal dreamer or ending a changeling\u2019s mortal life.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Willfully destroying a treasure or killing a greater creature of the Dreaming.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Violating a prohibition of the changeling\u2019s primary legacy. Breaking an oath.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Spending time in proximity to a being or area of high Banality. Failing to overcome Banality.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Chimerically killing another changeling or certain lesser chimera such as a child\u2019s imaginary friend.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Ravaging a mortal for glamour or destroying a piece of dross.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Participating in the routine of mortal society \u2013 going to school, holding down a job, being concerned about money, etc.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Passing up an opportunity to cultivate glamour or embark on a quest.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-27T23:37:50-07:00","id":151062,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">Could you expand on the Acts of Banality list? <br /> <br /> Superficially, it looks like you took the idea of a Path of Enlightenment from VtM and applied it to Banality; which is rather brilliant. \u2018Banality\u2019 always felt unnecessarily ambiguous and I think framing it from a \u2018Hierarchy of Sins\u2019 perspective would (help) overcome the (seemingly) ill-defined definitions of Banality. <br /> <br /> I\u2019m voting to see more. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T00:45:34-07:00","id":151102,"title":"","content":"\u2026and the \u201cstoryteller can add new ones or move these around as he sees fit\u201d clause keeps this nearly as flexible as the original approach that it\u2019s designed to replace. This is a good example of how to properly use a \u201chierarchy of sins\u201d.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T03:17:41-07:00","id":151180,"title":"","content":"Well, I never played C:tD, also I have neve read any book, or Forum-Thread about it (first time now) but yeah. What you wrote makes sense to me, its easy to read and understand and looks like a solid game-mechanic. I can't compare it to the old official rules cause I don't know them, but if you hadn't told us it was your take on banality I would've thought that must be the official rules 'cause it sounds well phrased and makes perfectly sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1235-eadee","name":"Eadee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T06:34:41-07:00","id":151255,"title":"","content":"I like it Pookaknight, i like it alot but i Think a redefining of dross and gradients of chimera would go hand in hand with it too, otherwise what may make for a good story (ie worthy of glamour) actually ends up powering Banality . Granted you do have the Storyteller can decide to add, remove, switch or ignore. I was thinking reading through my Glamour Economy thread and looking at what you've put here was that fulfilling Legacies could do a double duty for willpower / glamour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2767-moirdryd","name":"Moirdryd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T08:29:16-07:00","id":151318,"title":"","content":"While the concept is good I do think that there are few things that sound too much like Morality system instead of Banality system. What I mean is that it sounds rather odd that many things quite natural to Fae, and Changelings, seem to generate Banality. Epic Quest to slay a Dragon, slam Banality, acting like a Red Cap should, slam Banality, etc. Also unless you mean destroying Dross to mean destroying it in a way that doesn't allow you to gain Glamour from it sounds a bit problematic too, not only because Glamour economy is what it is but also because while Questing in the Deep or Far dreaming your very food is likely to be a form of Dross so...<br /> <br /> While I do see why you put these on the list, you think that a destruction of something chimerical means a little bit less magic all around, I disagree with the premise I just assumed you had. That is I think the Quests and fighting between the Chimerae and other critters of the Dreaming are the very fuel of the actual Dreaming. Yes you do loose a Dragon but you gain a story that will generate Glamour for a long time and encourage others to take up such Quests and to dream of them. Also it is what happens within the Dreaming all the time, and since Banality is totally alien and in fact poisonous to the Dreaming I find that actions that are natural to the Dreaming, such as a knight slaying a Dragon (or a Red Cap monster hunter doing the same) should not be things that generate Banality. Basically doing anything that belong into an epic tale shouldn't really generate Banality while doing things that could reduce an epic into the realm of the mundane should inflict Banality.<br /> <br /> And while this is only my opinion neither should participating in mortal society. like school, if you do so by bringing a touch of the fantastic with you to the otherwise mundane. But then again this has always been my problem with Banality as a mechanic it is entirely situational. By it's very nature only boring things should generate Banality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T21:50:56-07:00","id":152106,"title":"","content":"Interestingly, the only things on the chart that aren't in the core book as granting Banality are the two lowest levels. I actually broke apart the killing chimera prohibition so that killing some chimera isn't as banal as killing others. In both cases, I didn't intend for it to mean killing any chimera. I think it got worded fairly clearly for the lesser chimera, but I can see where I should have been clearer on the greater chimera. I wasn't intending for it to cover slaying a dragon to rescue the princess or hunting a great fell beast. I meant it to cover killing fixtures of the Dreaming, things like cutting down Yggdrasil, slaying Ouroboros, or murdering one of nine Muses. There are few other revisions I have in mind that are alluded to in this write up. Ravaging is an act of Banality because I want to see Reverie be just as valid for unseelie creativity as seelie. The part I thought would be the most controversial is how gaining Banality can age changelings. The seemings as they are now are problematic. I'd like to see them decoupled somewhat from a character's actual age. I actually took a lot of inspiration for the way it's structured from Demon: The Fallen. The Torment system always struck me as an evolution of Banality. I wanted Banality to feel like the result of the characters' actions instead of something random. As a bonus with a system like this, a character should actually be gaining Banality more slowly than by default, especially by the time they become a grump.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T21:54:20-07:00","id":152108,"title":"","content":"Hm, my last post wasn't one block of text when I posted it. Sorry for the terrible formatting! I'd go back and edit it, but the forum doesn't seem to want to let me do that from my phone.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T22:47:55-07:00","id":152159,"title":"","content":"Hmmmmm Pooka, I applaud your effort here. I am concerned a bit about that chart. I know of a Grump fae who regularly has to use his Banality to help others in the grips of Bedlam and has even used it to battle a Mage's Quiet to bring them back from madness before they destroy themselves and others. He's used it to save other Changelings and while he is a Grump and his Perm Banality is high, it is not so high as a 9. How would his actions of using Banality to help even if it harms place on that scale?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T01:03:50-07:00","id":152216,"title":"","content":"Wielding Banality itself always comes at a price. That's true even under the default system. This way, at least the character gets a roll to avoid gaining Banality. Under the actual rules, you can never counter magic with Banality or deny chimerical reality without first taking a temporary Banality point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T03:30:10-07:00","id":152301,"title":"","content":"Personally I think I would reverse the age/banality thing such that at low banality a changeling would always seem younger and more vital than there actual age, at the lower end perhaps even actually ageing slower but as it climbs age sets in and even catches up with them. I think that rather than try and tell us growing up sucks the theme should be &quot;We don't stop playing because we grow old; we grow old because we stop playing.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2939-avaron","name":"Avaron","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2939_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T04:04:01-07:00","id":152313,"title":"","content":"A good point. There is a merit like that though already. Faerie Eternity. Costs 4 points. While the changeling is aware and active, they age slower, but if they forget themselves the human ages like everyone else. What you propose Avaron would make that Merit redundant. But what you're saying seems to make sense to apply to all Fae.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T04:47:09-07:00","id":152362,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darling_Rose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/150899-pookaknight-s-changeling-palooza?p=152313#post152313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A good point. There is a merit like that though already. Faerie Eternity. Costs 4 points. While the changeling is aware and active, they age slower, but if they forget themselves the human ages like everyone else. What you propose Avaron would make that Merit redundant. But what you're saying seems to make sense to apply to all Fae.</div> </div> </div> </div>After Mages got their 1-point Unaging Merit I've always seen this one a bit overpriced especially since for Mages immortality is always achieved through Magic while for the Fae, and ergo the Changelings in my opinion, it has always been natural part of them, especially since they do not age while in Freeholds or Dreaming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T05:06:23-07:00","id":152370,"title":"","content":"Eh? Which book is that one in. I must have missed it. But then again I took the Mayfly curse for two characters just to challenge myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T15:36:31-07:00","id":153180,"title":"","content":"Immortality as in not ageing is usually extremely over priced in games, from a in character point I'd view it's very strong and valuable. But from a game play standpoint it's never going to come up, so you don't age great the campaign will be long over before that matters or you well get killed doing something. It's very nice for character history and lending a sense of wonder to a story but it just isn't all that useful when actual playing the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2939-avaron","name":"Avaron","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2939_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"28"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-29T20:48:45-07:00","id":153457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darling_Rose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/150899-pookaknight-s-changeling-palooza?p=152370#post152370\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh? Which book is that one in. I must have missed it. But then again I took the Mayfly curse for two characters just to challenge myself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Revised Core in the Merit section, under supernatural Merits if my memory serves me correctly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-04T19:02:38-07:00","id":159600,"title":"","content":"Now that I'm back in town and at a real computer again, it's time for more! First though, I want to thank everyone for their feedback on my alternate Banality rules, lots of good stuff there! I'll see what I can do to address or incorporate some of it.<br /> <br /> My next post is how I imagine Banality might have been described in an imaginary Changeling revised edition's setting chapter. It's written in first person as was often the case in the revised era. As the other revised editions did, it brings some of the metaplot front and center. It gives banality a, hopefully, clear definition, and specifically says that science and religion aren't banal in and of themselves. It also makes Banality less abstract, and gives PCs something tangible to struggle against. I like that it also really drives home that events in the Dreaming directly affect Earth and events on Earth directly affect the Dreaming. The two may be separate physically, but you still can't get away from your problems in one by fleeing to the other.<br /> <br /> As is the nature of the beast, I expect this one will be somewhat controversial. Without further ado: <hr />What is Banality? Some say Banality is the result of religion or science in our world. Those people claim that if we could just get rid of all of the churches and accountants, the gates to Arcadia would open, Earth and the Dreaming would reunite, and we would reclaim our lost glory. Those people are idiots.<br /> <br /> You can\u2019t tell me that all the music, art, and poetry inspired by religion are utterly devoid of glamour. If you don\u2019t believe me, look at the Piet\u00e0, the Blue Mosque, or anything from Tintoretto. If you need proof that science is inspirational, just try listening to the likes of Carl Sagan or Neil deGrasse Tyson without immediately wanting to explore the universe. For fuck\u2019s sake, the Resurgence would never have happened without the moon landing!<br /> <br /> No, Banality is far older than science or even religion.<br /> <br /> The original inhabitants of the Dreaming, the Fomorians, arose in a time before humanity. In that world there was no intelligence, no love, no hate, and no wonder or passion of any kind. Existence simply was. Creatures operated off of nothing more than primordial instinct and base survival urges. They lived short, brutal lives. They were born, ate, reproduced, and became food themselves. There wasn\u2019t anything to aspire to - nothing to gain, nothing to lose, nothing even to fear. Eternal apathetic subsistence \u2014 that is the Fomorian dream.<br /> <br /> That is Banality.<br /> <br /> Impossibly, humans and the Tuatha de Danaan emerged from the morass. Humans longed for more than mere existence. They were full of fear and wonder at the world around them. They loved and hated with equal passion. They built vast creations and waged terrible wars. The Tuatha formed us from humanity\u2019s boundless imagination.<br /> <br /> Like matter and antimatter, the Tuathan and Fomorian dreams could not coexist. A conflict over the fate of humanity raged known as the Tessarakonta, or, the War of Trees. In the end, we were victorious. The Fomorians were imprisoned, safely sequestered far away from Earth and the Dreaming. Humans were free to drive us just as we inspired them to new heights. However, our victory ultimately set the stage for our downfall.<br /> <br /> Triumphant, the Tuatha departed for realms unknown leaving us, the Kithain, as stewards over humanity and the Dreaming. In their absence, we forgot how precarious our being once was. Humanity\u2019s limitless creativity gave us the power of gods. We grew ever more enthralled in our own petty conflicts. We came to see humans as mere playthings, pawns to be used and discarded. Imperceptibly at first, human kind\u2019s imagination began to wane. Is it any wonder that with their fates beyond their control they turned to apathy? It all came to a head when, during a long forgotten dispute, we created a weapon so terrible it actually fractured the Fomorians\u2019 eternal prison.<br /> <br /> Banality came rushing back into the world. Humanity embraced it and used it as a weapon against us. They shattered the bonds between Earth and the Dreaming, cutting us off from our homeland. Since that time, Banality has only grown and multiplied. Today it saturates the world.<br /> <br /> You see it in the minimum wage worker working three jobs, living off food stamps and still not able to make ends meet. You see it in the corporate drone sitting all day in his cubicle only to come home and sit all night in front of his TV. You see it in the high flying corporate executive working for less than a year, fired for his incompetence, and awarded a golden parachute in the tens of millions. No one has time or incentive to dream anymore. The best anyone can hope for is to survive from one day to the next.<br /> <br /> Now we stand at the brink of the Endless Winter, a time when Banality grinds everything back down to mere existence and the Fomorian dream holds complete sway over Earth once more.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-04T20:00:11-07:00","id":159666,"title":"","content":"I don't think I'd classify the &quot;golden parachute&quot; example as <i>Banal</i>. An injustice, arguably; but it doesn't match the &quot;so busy struggling to survive that you don't have time to dream&quot; theme that you've got going.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-04T20:29:50-07:00","id":159686,"title":"","content":"I meant for it to fall under no incentive to dream, but rereading it after your comment, I can see what you mean.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-05T12:07:37-07:00","id":160126,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">PookaKnight:<br /> <br /> In the Banality \u2018Hierarchy of Sins,\u2019 you have [for Banality 4] <b>\u201cChimerically killing another changeling or certain lesser chimera such as a child\u2019s imaginary friend,\u201d</b>:<br /> <br /> Would you offer any leniency, in your games, to Kithians that embody this sort of thing? For example, would a redcap accrue Banality from [chimerically] munching on a nocker (cacafuego notwithstanding)?</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-05T12:46:46-07:00","id":160156,"title":"","content":"It's something that gives Banality in the actual Changeling rules. It's also something I usually ignore, because, like you say, there are plenty of valid character types that wouldn't have a problem with chimerically killing. It might even seem Glamourous due to the nature of the story.<br /> <br /> I wanted the Acts of Banality chart to hew as close to the actual rules as possible while making Banality gains feel more like a consequence and less of an arbitrary punishment from the ST. I left it in for three reasons. First, the act of chimerically killing another changeling does actually diminish Glamour in the world, even if only temporarily most of the time. Second, it's low on the Banality chart. A starting Grump would never even have to test against it, which makes sense. Grumps are the veterans of life - they've adventured, gone to war, made the difficult decisions, taken their lumps, and have the scars to show for it. They know the actual price of the glories a Wilder might be chomping at the bit to embark upon. Finally, the character is allowed a roll to resist the Banality gain, unlike the actual rules.<br /> <br /> For example, a Childling would have to check for a Banality gain after chimerically killing someone. However, odds are very good they won't gain Banality from any particular act. Childlings mostly have to worry about Banality from the frequency of times they'll have to check for it. Eventually, they're going to get some bad rolls.<br /> <br /> So yeah, if I were to use the Acts of Banality chart in a game, I'd probably leave that it in. Over all, I think it's more thematic than it isn't thematic. Characters that do a bit of chimerical killing are still pretty viable since they can resist the Banality gains and even become immune to them at a certain point, which isn't the case in the actual rules.<br /> <br /> As an aside, Nicky and Jackie, the former Changeling developers during Changeling's Arthaus era, were asked a similar question about Banality and chimerically killing others after War in Concordia came out. Their answer has always stuck with me. To paraphrase them, if a changeling is planning on going to war or a chimerical killing spree, they better plan on having a reliable source of Glamour to offset their Banality.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T11:04:04-07:00","id":185632,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica Neue\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Recent political issues have got me thinking about Changeling.<br /> <br /> Classic World of Darkness has always worn its politics on its sleeve. That's not a complaint - its part of what makes the setting so vibrant and engaging. The interesting thing is, 20 years later, those issues are still affecting us. For example, things in Werewolf that were grossly over exaggerated satire became, sadly, almost prophetic. Now, between the great recession and recent SCOTUS rulings, the problems of Changeling are more immediate than ever before. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica Neue\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Earlier in this thread, I posted a write up of Banalty. The point was to show how Banality is relevant and immediate, more than just simply nostalgia for childhood. I tied it to the Fomorians and made it an active force in the world. To do so, I used the issues we're all facing from the great recession.</span></span></span><ul><li>No one can dare to dream when they're working multiple jobs and still struggling to put food on the table.</li> <li>The only solace when you can't follow your life long passion because no one is hiring is mindless escape so you can't think about it.</li> <li>Corporate executives have no incentive to dream big or find creative solutions when they get rewarded in the tens of millions for running a company into the ground instead. It also directly leads to the other two points.</li> <li>Taken together, they all result in a world of passionless subsistence and dull mediocrity.</li> </ul><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica Neue\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">If I were to do it over again, I'd include the recent SCOTUS rulings. Citizens United is a clear victory for Banality to get it's status quo entrenched. The Hobby Lobby case institutionalizes corporate religion trumping an individual's beliefs, forcing them to conform.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica Neue\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">I dearly hope there is a 20th Anniversary Edition. Now more than ever before, Changeling: The Dreaming has much to say.</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":648,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","user":"PookaKnight","date":".  clarity 07-01-2014, 02:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-01T23:51:33-07:00","id":186077,"title":"","content":"With all due respect \u2014 which is considerable \u2014 I disagree. Banality shouldn\u2019t be primarily about class warfare, with the tendency not to follow your dreams being attributable to too much or too little money. Closer to the heart of Banality, IMHO, is <i>red tape</i>, or excessive bureaucracy (both corporate and government) that insulates decision-makers from the people their decisions affect (making it easier to be heartless) and shackles everyone involved with regulations that are often well-intentioned but ultimately strangle the human spirit. The root cause isn\u2019t greed; it\u2019s a tendency to meddle in other peoples\u2019 lives. The soul-crushing effects of poverty can be in there, too; but not as the primary source of Banality.<br /> <br /> I\u2019d also like to see <i>less</i> in the way of politics in cWoD games. As a conservative, I\u2019ve found that there\u2019s a lot that I like in said games; but when they get preachy and start identifying conservative causes with the source of a given game\u2019s primary antagonists (e.g., the Weaver and/or the Wyrm in <b>Werewolf</b>, or Banality in <b>Changeling</b>), it hinders my ability to enjoy the game by shattering my willing suspension of disbelief: such portrayals of conservatives clash severely with my personal experience. And I get enough self-righteous indignation from liberals in real life; I don\u2019t need it in my hobby as well. To the extent that politics <i>do</i> show up, I prefer them to remain squarely in the realm of IC opinions rather than being presented as OOC statements of \u201cfact\u201d.<br /> <br /> Of course, I also tend to view the current political climate as needlessly antagonistic, with a tendency to think that if group A disgrees with group B\u2019s <i>methods</i>, they must also disagree with their <i>goals</i> as well, leading to group B demonizing group A.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 07-02-2014, 03:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T06:58:50-07:00","id":186258,"title":"","content":"The idea behind the three examples is to show that Banality cuts across class lines. They all three have their souls calcified and their dreams torn asunder, just for different reasons and circumstances. Banality might use one to start a domino effect, but going against that one won't actually help anything.<br /> <br /> I don't see it as class warfare. I see it as examples of how anyone from all walks of life are smothered by Banality.<br /> <br /> If it still seems like certain classes are being vilified, perhaps it's a clever ploy by the White Court to strengthen the Red Court so they too may break their bonds and enter the world once more <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-02T20:18:35-07:00","id":186783,"title":"","content":"I hate to double post, but I want to get this one out here before I head out of town again for a little while - a modified version of the Nature realm. This is one I've been thinking about for years. I tried to make it scale appropriately while still being useful at only one dot. I also tried to keep it as close to the original as I could while filling in the types of natural occurrences that went unaddressed. Here it is:<br /> <br /> <b>Nature</b><br /> This realm encompasses the whole of the natural world, including the elements, flora and fauna.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"width: 500px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">Level</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Title</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Description</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Base Element</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Any nonliving, naturally occurring material consisting of the elements of the periodic table or the classical elements of earth, air, fire, and water. If the target has been worked in any way, it falls under Prop.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Verdant Forest</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Any living vegetation.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Feral Animal</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Any living, non-sapient creature.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Natural Phenomena</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Any natural occurrence such as weather, volcanic activity, earthquakes, etc.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">Laws of the Firmament</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\">The fundamental essences of nature such as gravity, light, quantum mechanics, etc.</td> </tr> </table></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}